Classic old-fashioned bakery-style ginger cookies. Creaming the butter and sugar for a full 5–7 minutes creates a puffy, tender cookie that stays soft for days.

*Adapted from [Taste of Home](https://www.tasteofhome.com/recipes/big-soft-ginger-cookies/print/).*

## Ingredients

- ¾ cup unsalted butter, softened to room temperature
- 1 cup granulated sugar (plus ¼ cup extra for rolling)
- 1 large egg, room temperature
- ¼ cup dark molasses
- 2 ¼ cups all-purpose flour
- 2 teaspoons ground ginger
- 1 teaspoon baking soda
- ¾ teaspoon ground cinnamon
- ½ teaspoon ground cloves
- ¼ teaspoon salt

## Instructions

1. **Whip the Butter and Sugar:** In a large bowl or using a stand mixer fitted with the paddle, beat the room-temperature softened butter with 1 cup granulated sugar on medium speed. Keep mixing for 5 to 7 minutes; aerating this mixture thoroughly creates the airy, tender foundation needed for soft cookies.
2. **Incorporate Egg and Molasses:** Drop in the room-temperature egg followed by the dark molasses. Blend on medium-low until the wet base is completely smooth and uniform in color, pausing to wipe down the bowl edges.
3. **Combine the Spiced Flour Mixture:** In a separate medium vessel, whisk together the flour, ground ginger, baking soda, cinnamon, cloves, and salt so the spices and leavener are distributed evenly.
4. **Bring the Dough Together:** Pour the dry blend into the creamed butter in two additions, mixing on low speed just until streaks of dry flour vanish. Avoid overmixing once combined.
5. **Shape and Sugar-Coat:** Scoop out portions of dough and roll them between your palms into 1 ½-inch spheres. Pour the extra granulated sugar into a small bowl and roll each sphere around until completely coated in glistening crystals.
6. **Bake to Soft Perfection:** Position dough spheres 2 inches apart on baking sheets (ungreased or lined with parchment). Bake at 350°F (175°C) for 10 to 12 minutes. The cookies will puff up and show delicate crinkles on top while remaining very tender in their centers. Avoid overbaking to keep them chewy.
7. **Rest on Wire Racks:** Allow the warm cookies to firm up on the sheet pans for 2 minutes, then transfer them using a spatula to wire cooling racks to cool completely.

## Notes

- **Secret to Softness:** The generous creaming time (5 to 7 minutes) aerates the butter base and prevents the cookies from baking up dense or flat.
- **Storage:** Keep in an airtight container with a slice of sandwich bread; they will stay soft and chewy for over a week.
